{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c416de3\" lines=\"99-118\">\n<description>\nReplicate a pre-drawn corner pattern to every other marker-bearing corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most frequent color. Hollow 0-frames (possibly partial L-shapes); each\n  frame-corner cell is either 0 or a painted (non-bg, non-0) color.\n- >= 1 'source' corner: the pattern is already drawn (one color, both outward\n  cells and an inward-diagonal trail).\n- Other corners: a single colored pixel on the inward diagonal at the pattern's\n  deepest (k,k) offset; the pixel's color is that corner's target color.\n\nRule:\n- Extract the canonical (TL-oriented) pattern from any source.\n- For each non-source corner whose marker cell is colored, paint the same\n  pattern rotated to that corner's orientation, using the marker's color.\n\nKey insight: the pattern is symmetric about the corner's inward-diagonal axis\n(canonical dr == dc), so it reflects cleanly across all four corner orientations.\nPainting uses each target's OWN marker color, not the source color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c9680e90\" lines=\"52-67\">\n<description>\nMove each mirrored bottom source pixel to the far end of its trail and redraw the top half as its reflection.\n\nInput structure\n- A solid non-background row separates equal-height top and bottom halves.\n- The top half has one marker color on the background.\n- In the bottom half, exactly one color has positions that mirror the top markers across the separator, and the other color forms trails touching those source pixels.\n\nRule\n- Identify the bottom source color by reflecting each bottom color into the top half and matching the top marker set.\n- For every bottom source pixel, follow its unique 4-connected trail of the other bottom color to the trail endpoint.\n- Clear the old top markers, bottom sources, and trails; place the source color at each endpoint and place the top marker color at the reflected endpoint.\n\nKey insight\n- The top half is only a mirror display of the bottom moving sources; the actual motion is encoded by the trail attached to each bottom source pixel.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"faa9f03d\" lines=\"36-52\">\n<description>\nRepair a one-cell-thick colored line network by replacing singleton markers, filling short orthogonal gaps, and redirecting yellow-marked arms to the edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid with a dominant background.\n- Line colors form one-cell-thick horizontal/vertical stroke fragments.\n- Marker colors are exactly those whose 4-connected components are all singleton pixels.\n- Red singleton markers are missing line cells; yellow singleton markers are directional controls next to a line endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Assign every marker to the nearest visible line color in row/column line-of-sight, preferring an endpoint supported along that same axis.\n- For each line color, fill the gap between consecutive facing endpoints in a row or column.\n- A yellow control erases the old straight arm on the far side of its endpoint and paints the same color from that endpoint through the control pixel to the grid edge.\n- At crossings, yellow-control rays win; otherwise an original-vs-filled crossing uses the more fragmented pre-repair line color, while pure filled crossings use the lower color id.\n\nKey insight: The singleton markers identify which sparse stroke fragments belong together; after they are colored, the intended lines are the axis-aligned gaps between facing endpoints, with yellow controls flipping one arm to the marked side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84ba50d3\" lines=\"36-54\">\n<description>\nDrop the blue sprites downward relative to the red divider, with vertical sprites passing through to the bottom.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One non-background color forms a single full-width horizontal divider row.\n- The other non-background color forms 4-connected sprites above the divider.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite pixels.\n- A one-column sprite falls to the bottom of its column and deletes the divider\n  pixel it crossed.\n- Any wider sprite falls until its unique widest horizontal row is immediately\n  above the divider row; if the moved sprite overlaps the divider, it covers it.\n\nKey insight:\nThe red row is not a generic floor: only one-column sprites pass through to the\nbottom, while wider sprites are registered by their widest horizontal segment.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c7f57c3e\" lines=\"22-35\">\n<description>\nSwap every sprite to the opposite prototype while preserving scale and position.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Two distinct small sprite patterns (prototypes) appear at various scales.\n- Each sprite instance is a scaled copy of one of the two prototypes, placed on the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the two prototype patterns from the smallest-area sprites.\n- For each sprite, determine which prototype it matches and its integer scale factor.\n- Replace it with the other prototype at the same scale, aligned by an internal anchor region (color-1 pixels).\n\nKey insight: Every sprite toggles to the alternate prototype; the anchor sub-region inside each sprite pins the alignment so swapped sprites land in the correct position regardless of scale.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dd6b8c4b\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nMove the closest reachable maroon pixels into the 3×3 block around the unique red center.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = orange floor, magenta walls, scattered maroon (9) pixels.\n- A unique red (2) pixel marks the center of a 3×3 destination block.\n\nRule:\n- Maroon pixels connected to the destination through floor/maroon cells (not blocked by walls) are candidates.\n- Rank candidates by distance to the destination; the closest ones fill the block in row-major order.\n- Moved maroons are erased (replaced with floor color).\n\nKey insight: Wall barriers restrict connectivity — only maroon pixels reachable via flood-fill through the floor qualify, so proximity alone isn't enough.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2a28add5\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nPaint each pink control pixel as a teal horizontal bar whose reach equals the foreground counts on that row.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one or more pink control pixels.\n- Each marker row has exactly one pink pixel; all other non-background pixels\n  in that row are counters.\nRule\n- Start from an all-background output grid.\n- For each pink pixel, count non-background pixels strictly to its left and\n  strictly to its right in the same row.\n- Paint teal from c - left_count through c + right_count on that row.\nKey insight\n- The foreground pixels are not copied; only their counts on each side of\n  each pink marker encode the teal bar length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e048c9ed\" lines=\"11-26\">\n<description>\nAdd a quantity-color label in the top marker column for each horizontal bar.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a black background and one non-background marker pixel in the top row.\n- Below the marker row, every non-empty source row contains one solid monochrome horizontal bar to the left of the marker column.\n- Source rows are staggered every other row.\n\nRule\n- Copy the input grid.\n- For each source bar, read its quantity as the bar length.\n- Paint the quantity color in the marker column on the same row as the bar:\n  2->1, 3->4, 4->9, 5->6.\n\nKey insight\n- The grey marker supplies the result column, while each bar supplies a small quantity with a fixed color code.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f8de559\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nErase the first border pixel hit by extending the red marker away from its pink trail.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds a unique red marker and a four-pixel\n  pink trail.\n- The pink pixels form one straight 8-connected ray starting next to the\n  red marker.\n- In the opposite direction from that trail, the red marker faces a\n  non-background border or obstacle pixel beyond background cells.\n\nRule\n- Keep the whole grid fixed except for the first non-background pixel on\n  the ray from the red marker away from the pink trail.\n- Recolor that one hit pixel to the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The pink trail marks where the red marker came from, so the edit is found\n  by ray-casting from the red marker in the opposite direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"abc82100\" lines=\"70-86\">\n<description>\nUse local source-target-pattern examples to replace matching source pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with isolated colored pixels and teal (8) pattern components.\n- A rule example is a 4-neighbor colored pair source->target whose continued\n  direction reaches an anchor touching a teal pattern component.\n\nRule:\n- For each rule example, copy that teal component onto every other pixel of\n  source.color, recoloring the copied component to target.color.\n- Translation maps the rule anchor onto the source pixel being replaced.\n- Remove teal components and all pixels used by rule examples.\n\nKey insight: The two adjacent colored pixels define the color substitution and\nthe anchor point; the nearby teal component is the stencil that gets recolored.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5a719d11\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nWithin each horizontal pair of two-color panels, swap the motif masks and recolor each pasted mask with the source panel's base color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A separator color forms full blank rows and at least one full blank column.\n- The non-separator area is split into row bands, each containing exactly two same-shaped rectangular panels.\n- Each panel has a dominant base color and one minority motif color.\n\nRule:\n- For every row band, clear each panel to its own base color.\n- Paste the right panel's motif mask into the left panel using the right base color.\n- Paste the left panel's motif mask into the right panel using the left base color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The motif colors themselves are distractors; only each motif's shape mask and the two panel base colors are exchanged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7491f3cf\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nComposite two pattern tiles into a fourth panel using flood-fill from an indicator pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- Four 5×5 panels in one row, border-separated.\n- Panel 0: cue pixels = wall boundary (8-connected, edge-to-edge, through center)\n           + one 8-isolated indicator pixel.\n- Panels 1–2: source patterns. Panel 3: blank (to fill).\n\nRule:\n- Flood-fill (4-conn) from indicator, blocked by wall → inside region.\n- Inside → non-bg from panel 1; outside → non-bg from panel 2.\n- Wall → panel 1 if non-bg; panel 2 fallback when wall is 4-connected.\n\nKey insight: The cue panel's isolated indicator and wall partition the tile into inside/outside masks that select between the two source panels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ca952ad\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nDrop every large foreground object straight down to the bottom while leaving small marker objects fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Objects of area at most three are stationary markers; larger objects are moving sprites.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each moving sprite from its original position.\n- Translate each moving sprite vertically downward until its bbox bottom reaches the grid bottom.\n- Repaint each translated sprite in its original color; small markers stay unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
